WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:18.539
After the October 2019 elections, in which
Mauricio Macri ended his presidency with a

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:22.250
gruelling defeat, Alberto Fernández was sworn
in as president on December 10th.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:29.010
And by his side, was Cristina Fernández de
Kirchner as Vice President.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.050
After four years where it looked like change
could be imminent, Argentina went back to

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.050
square one.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.190
The new President now faces two challenges
that seem more complicated to achieve than

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.050
winning the Paris Dakar rally with a Model
T Ford:

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.970
Number one on the list is to try to get the
country out of the tough, tough crisis it’s

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:46.970
going through.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:50.480
And second, to consolidate his power under
the shadow of his Vice President.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.540
Because, a new political war seems to be brooding
at the core of Kirchnerism.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:12.790
[Text: “Because of Argentina’s madness,
because of our madness we sometimes distance

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.271
ourselves and then come together again knowing
there were no vital differences between us."]

00:01:14.271 --> 00:01:16.420
And this brings up all sorts of questions.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.390
What were Fernández’s first measures after
he came to power?

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:24.030
What steps does the new president plan on
taking to get Argentina out of the ditch?

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.570
Will his plan help?

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:32.049
What lies ahead for the gaucho country?

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:37.679
Listen up.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:42.009
(A TAINTED LEGACY)

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:48.340
Yes, I know this is something politicians
often promise but you can't build a house

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.049
by starting with the roof.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:55.090
[Text: “Many people asked me to declare
on the state in which I had found the country

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.159
and I didn’t.

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:59.749
I think it is more constructive to do it today.”]

00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:04.340
If we want to analyze the task that Fernández
has ahead, first we need to understand the

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.900
country he was handed.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:10.320
And the truth is that the situation that the
new president has found himself in isn’t

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.180
exactly a relaxing tropical cruise.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.260
Not at all.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.870
Alberto Fernández got handed one of the worst
scenarios that a politician can imagine.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.050
We’ve already talked about it in lots of
videos, which I’ll leave links for in the

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:26.050
description.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:33.270
But here are three pieces of information to
summarize this apparent race towards the abyss:

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:39.650
Firstly, in 2019 inflation closed at more
than 50%, this number is equivalent to more

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.260
than 10 years of inflation in a normal country.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.069
Second, the Argentine economy is in recession,
one of the greatest economic recessions in

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:50.200
the world, where some sectors have dropped
by a whopping 10%.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:55.760
Third, the US dollar, which had an exchange
rate of 42 pesos at the start of 2019, ended

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.060
the year at almost 80 pesos in the free market.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.130
As you can see, a major, total and absolute
disaster.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:08.140
Because the so-called gradualism of previous
leader Macri was so gradual that some are

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.460
still looking for it.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.030
The only thing he achieved was a colossal
debt pyramid.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:19.489
Because in the end gradualism basically consisted
of the following:

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:22.950
Not cutting public spending and just making
small adjustments in order to attract more

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.209
investments that would make the economy grow.

00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:29.690
With this strategy, the burden of public debt
on the GDP would be reduced over the years,

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:32.040
but the debt amount itself would not be reduced.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:37.110
At the same time, any public deficits would
be bankrolled with more and more debt.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:40.319
And that’s exactly what happened:

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:44.909
Public spending and debt went down in GDP
terms while the public sector continued to

00:03:44.909 --> 00:03:45.909
grow.

00:03:45.909 --> 00:03:52.620
As you can see in this graph, year after year
the public deficit just kept increasing.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.349
(CHART)

00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:59.930
The difference between income and expenses
was basically paid with debt, which created

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:06.040
a huge debt balloon that finally burst, forcing
Argentina to once again go running to the

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.769
International Monetary Fund.

00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:14.551
Basically, in the end, as many economic and
political analysts have argued, the great

00:04:14.551 --> 00:04:20.010
difference between Kirchnerism and Macrism
was more a matter of form than substance,

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.900
because their policies were practically the
same.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.860
It seems that the party doesn’t make much
difference, all Argentine politicians seem

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.319
to share the same motto:

00:04:29.319 --> 00:04:32.030
“More taxes, more spending, more controls”.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:36.190
Over and over again.

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:37.639
And given the results...

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:41.400
Well the truth is that knowing exactly what
they’re looking for isn’t really clear.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:47.350
(AUDIO: “If we take the resources destined
to what is called social spending we see that

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:54.030
from 1983, when democracy returned, until
2018, the resources destined for so-called

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:59.830
social expenditure increased by 10 percent
of the GDP and poverty kept growing or remained

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.150
at very high levels.”

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:02.150
Roberto Cachanosky)

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:03.150
(CHART)

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.819
You saw it with Macri, social spending reached
record numbers during the same period that

00:05:05.819 --> 00:05:07.890
poverty levels shot up.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:14.650
(December 2019: According to the Social Debt
Observatory of the Catholic University of

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:20.090
Argentina, poverty in Argentina reached 40.8%
and is the highest of the decade.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:27.199
These indices imply that 16 million Argentines
live in poverty and 3.6 million in extreme

00:05:27.199 --> 00:05:28.199
poverty.

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:29.199
INFOBAE)

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:32.810
To give you a better idea, this country currently
has 8 million workers who pay taxes to support

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:37.520
20 million people who directly or indirectly
receive a check from the State, whether at

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.620
the national, provincial or municipal level.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.740
Obviously, this isn’t feasible.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.710
But... let's look at it another way: Who in
their right mind would invest a single dollar

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:50.540
in a country with such conditions?

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:54.810
That, my friends, is the question that the
new government is going to have to face.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.699
But, hold on, do you think I'm exaggerating?

00:05:56.699 --> 00:06:00.820
Well... check the analysis that the Taxpayers
Association prepared: in Argentina, more than

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:06.120
half of the price of a car consists of taxes.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:13.580
Specifically, on average, 54.8% of the price
of a new car consists of taxes.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:14.580
More than 50%.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:15.580
(CHART)

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.160
And this is just for cars that are half manufactured
and sold within Argentina, because if we talk

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:24.210
about imported and high-end cars, the taxes
would rise even more… as well as the price.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.300
This is why, in Argentina, a high-end car
can cost up to three times more than it would

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:32.750
in the United States and more than twice what
it costs in a European Union country – countries

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:36.530
where car sales also have significant taxes.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:42.410
And that helps to explain why buying a late
model car in Argentina, like the ones found

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.080
in any country in the developed world, isn’t
an option for everyone.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.150
But that’s not all.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:52.379
We can also illustrate the point by looking
at a typical shopping cart.

00:06:52.379 --> 00:06:57.510
According to the Argentine Institute of Fiscal
Analysis, more than 40, 40% of the price of

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:05.120
the usual food and non-alcoholic beverages
that people buy consists of taxes.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.009
At this point the burning questions are:

00:07:07.009 --> 00:07:10.600
Faced with such a scenario, what measures
has Fernández taken?

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.240
What strategy has he chosen?

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.510
Listen up.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:22.430
[Text: “I ask you to remember the past so
we don’t repeat it.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:27.350
Because this is a different time.”]

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:32.580
(THE NEW GOVERNMENT’S PATH)

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:37.370
Given this situation; with Argentina drowning
in the third highest inflation rate in the

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:40.660
world, with an economy that’s in free fall
and a huge national debt...

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.690
What measures has Fernandez taken?

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.360
Has he decided to end the deficit once and
for all?

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.540
Has he decided to approve new incentives so
foreign companies will invest in the country?

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.419
Well, not exactly.

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:58.080
The new government has approved a very harsh
adjustment, perhaps more severe than expected.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:03.599
The problem is that this adjustment, once
again, doesn’t include the public sector.

00:08:03.599 --> 00:08:08.680
While the private sector is going to take
a considerable blow...

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.950
No one will touch the public sector.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:16.699
No cuts to administrative expenses, or political
expenses, nor bureaucratic expenses.

00:08:16.699 --> 00:08:22.110
So, what does this adjustment really consist
of?

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:24.520
Well... let’s take a look at the main measures:

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:32.900
[Text: “Those of us who embrace the Peronist
cause have come to be the voice of those who

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.960
have no voice.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:40.440
To give rights to those who have none.”]

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.420
First of all, they’re raising taxes again.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:48.810
Rates are raised, withholding taxes on agricultural
exports are increased - this is a kind of

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:53.420
export tax for a very important sector in
Argentina – and, most importantly, the non-taxable

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.170
amounts on personal taxes won’t be updated.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:00.970
The latter, considering that the country has
an inflation rate of more than 50%, is something

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:06.510
that amounts to a huge fiscal bite for taxpayers
with a lower income.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:09.700
But... let me explain this better.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:14.930
You see, in every country, taxes, especially
personal taxes, tend to have exemptions, so

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.740
if you earn less than a certain amount you
don’t have to pay.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:24.890
Well, in Argentina not updating this “minimum”
considering the increases in inflation will

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:27.300
come as a tough blow to many people.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.440
Imagine for example – and this is an example,
that a freelancer, a professional, has an

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:34.880
exemption threshold of one million pesos,
so everything he or she earns above that amount

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.010
is taxed.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:43.040
As inflation sits at more than 50%, imagine
that this professional’s income last year

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.750
increased by 30%, to 1,300,000 pesos.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:50.360
This professional is actually poorer than
the previous year, because his or her income

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:54.790
has increased less than the rate of inflation,
so they won’t be able to buy as much with

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:57.290
the money.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:04.130
However, on top of that taxes need to be paid
on the 300 thousand pesos, as they exceed

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:08.710
the exemption threshold ... which means the
take home pay is even lower and buying power

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.870
is weakened even more.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:16.980
Well, that’s exactly what is going to happen
in Argentina: higher tax rates and a non-updated

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:20.350
minimum tax threshold mean more fiscal pressure.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:26.839
But it doesn't end there.

00:10:26.839 --> 00:10:30.350
Another group that is really going to feel
the adjustment is retirees.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:34.490
Given the high inflation rates, the Macri
government established a kind of semi-annual

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.260
adjustment, so that pensioners wouldn’t
lose so much purchasing power.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:42.560
Well, the new president has decided to freeze
that update for 180 days for all retirees

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:44.870
who earn more than $250 per month.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.060
And, of course, given the enormous inflation
that exists in the country, this will mean

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:51.560
a very significant loss in purchasing power.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.200
In other words, a harsh blow to the wallets
of retirees.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:59.040
And... that’s not the end of it.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.570
Personal property tax, which is something
like a wealth tax, has also been raised.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:06.610
And, hold on because in Argentina the law
considers everyone who has assets worth more

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:09.020
than $30,000 to be rich.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:11.100
Thirty thousand dollars!

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:12.490
That’s crazy.

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:17.970
But perhaps, out of all the recent measures,
the most controversial one is the new policy

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:19.209
regarding the dollar.

00:11:19.209 --> 00:11:23.370
Fernández’s government has imposed a 30%
surcharge for all people who want to save

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:26.959
in dollars, or who simply want to buy products
or services abroad.

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:30.760
This is known as the solidarity dollar.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:38.300
That way, even though the official exchange
rate is at around 60 pesos per dollar, if

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:43.149
you want to buy dollars to travel abroad or
to import a camera, the exchange rate applied

00:11:43.149 --> 00:11:46.620
to the transaction will be over 80 pesos per
dollar.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:50.579
You want to travel, invest or buy things abroad...
then you have to pay for the privilege.

00:11:50.579 --> 00:11:52.829
That’s just the way things are.

00:11:52.829 --> 00:11:55.230
It’s quite terrible.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:58.440
As if this weren’t enough, the amount of
dollars that you can officially buy is capped

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:00.110
at 200 dollars per person.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:03.860
So, if you want more than that, you’ll have
to exchange your money on the streets, in

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:06.230
the black market.

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:10.269
But... let's take a moment to think about
all this.

00:12:10.269 --> 00:12:14.790
Even though in principle companies aren’t
affected, imagine a small Argentine professional

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.420
who exports products worth ten thousand dollars.

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:21.050
When this money enters the country, they will
exchange it at the official exchange rate,

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:22.630
which is 600,000 pesos.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:26.970
Now assume that that same professional wants
to use that money to buy some equipment in,

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:29.370
say, Germany, which costs another 10,000 dollars.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.370
Well, then he has a problem.

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:34.610
When he goes to exchange his money back to
dollars, he’ll get the solidarity dollar

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:40.380
exchange rate, which is 30% more expensive,
so his 600 thousand pesos will only get him

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:42.120
7,500 dollars.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.790
And not only that, if he wants to go ahead
with that transaction, his company will have

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:49.470
to go to the black market because he won’t
be able to buy more than 200 dollars at the

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:54.040
bank.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:58.420
So here’s the question, do you think this
is the best way to attract investment and

00:12:58.420 --> 00:12:59.880
grow businesses?

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.940
It really doesn't seem to bode well.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:08.370
It’s true that with these adjustments, the
government is sending a clear message to the

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:12.170
international markets that Argentina wants
to pay, will pay its debts and therefore will

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:16.200
be able to renew its credits and even obtain
more international financing.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:20.089
But the problem is that if it doesn’t touch
public spending and only punishes the private

00:13:20.089 --> 00:13:27.710
economy even more, Argentina runs the risk
of drowning completely.

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:29.860
This is not an easy situation, that’s clear.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:34.670
But hey, maybe politicians could start the
adjustment by setting an example, right?

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:39.540
Check out the calculation that Roberto Cachanosky
developed based on the 2019 budgets.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:40.540
Take a look.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:41.540
[Monthly cost per senator]

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.540
Argentina: 188,000 euros per month
Spain: 17,540 euros per month

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:43.540
Monthly cost per legislator

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:44.540
Argentina: 53,727 euros per month
Spain: 21,700 euros per month

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:45.670
Calculated on 23/12/2019 on the 2019 budget.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:46.810
2019 average exchange rate: $ 56.46 per euro.]

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:48.480
Anyway, that’s how things stand in Argentina.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:53.050
Alberto Fernández’s government has begun
his mandate with some harsh adjustments...

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.259
adjustments that follow the Argentinian tradition.

00:13:55.259 --> 00:13:58.420
Could it be that Argentine politicians don’t
know any better?

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.700
In VisualPolitik we’ll be keeping an eye
on how events unfold.

